="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cüneyd Bey</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Cüneyd Bey was a Turkish lord who held Smyrna and fought to restore the Aydinid beylik in the decades after Timur's victory over the Ottomans in 1402.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/cunigunde-of-luxembourg"><span class="enc-name">Cunigunde of Luxembourg</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Cunigunde of Luxembourg was the wife of Emperor Henry II and was crowned empress in 1014.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cunimund"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cunimund.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cunimund</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Cunimund was the last king of the Gepids, ruling from about 560 from the region of Sirmium.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cunipert"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cunipert.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cunipert</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Cunipert was King of the Lombards, co-ruler with his father Perctarit from 680 and sole king from 688 to 700.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cunobelinus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cunobelinus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cunobelinus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Cunobelinus was a king of the Catuvellauni who ruled much of south-eastern Britain from about 9 to 40 CE from his capital at Camulodunum, modern Colchester.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cuntisuyu"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cuntisuyu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cuntisuyu</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Cuntisuyu was the south-western quarter of the Inca Empire, reaching from Cusco to the Pacific coast of southern Peru.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cupbearer"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cupbearer.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cupbearer</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">A cupbearer was a court official who served drinks to the king, a position of trust and access to the ruler.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cupids"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cupids.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cupids</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Cupids, originally Cuper's Cove, is a town on Conception Bay in Newfoundland, Canada.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cupisnique"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cupisnique.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cupisnique</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">Cupisnique was a culture of the north coast of Peru, flourishing from about 1500 to 500 BCE in valleys such as Jequetepeque, Chicama and Moche.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cura-annonae"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cura-annonae.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cura Annonae</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The cura annonae was the Roman system for supplying grain to the city of Rome, including the free distribution of grain, the annona, to citizens.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/cures"><span class="enc-name">Cures</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Cures was an ancient town of the Sabines north-east of Rome, near modern Fara in Sabina.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/curicaueri"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/curicaueri.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Curicaueri</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Curicaueri was the fire and sun god of the Purépecha of western Mexico, patron of the ruling Uacúsecha lineage and of the Purépecha state.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/curse-of-agade"><span class="enc-name">Curse of Agade</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Curse of Agade is a Sumerian literary composition, probably of the late 3rd millennium BCE, explaining the fall of the city of Akkad.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cursus-publicus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cursus-publicus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cursus publicus</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The cursus publicus was the state courier and transport service of the Roman Empire, established by Augustus, with relay stations along the main roads.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/curt-von-francois"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/curt-von-francois.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Curt von François</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Curt von François was a German army officer and explorer in Africa.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/curtis-island"><span class="enc-name">Curtis Island</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Curtis Island is a large island off the coast of central Queensland, Australia, near the port of Gladstone.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cusco"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cusco.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cusco</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Cusco is a city in the Andes of southern Peru, about 3,400 metres above sea level, which was the capital of the Inca Empire.</span></a></li></ul>
